## Why This Is Asked
Tests a branchless, efficient 128-bit bit-count using a proven 64-bit SWAR approach on two halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Treat 128-bit as two independent 64-bit parts
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// C-like helper (conceptual)
static inline uint8_t pop64(uint64_t v){
  v -= (v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
uint8_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of bit pattern encoding, branchless 64-bit manipulation, and mask-driven bit dilation techniques. It evaluates consistency across endianness and fixed-width arithmetic skills.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless bit manipulation
- Fixed-width masks for progressive dilation
- 64-bit hexadecimal mask constants
- Bit-level reasoning and positional mapping

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t spread32to64(uint32_t x) {
    uint64_t y = x;

    // Progressive dilation using fixed masks
    y = (y | (y << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
    y = (y | (y << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
    y = (y | (y << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    y = (y | (y << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
    y = (y | (y << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

    // Duplicate each bit to adjacent position
    return y | (y << 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

